What This Feature Does
User clicks "Import Paper → Generate Model" in Eclipse → selects PDF → chooses LLM provider → pipeline runs → .compmodel opens in Sirius diagram.
Concrete Implementation Steps
Step 0: Create a Python wrapper script (run_full_pipeline.py)
This combines Phase 2 + Phase 3 into a single callable:
# AI-ASSISTED MODEL-DRIVEN EPIDEMIOLOGY/run_full_pipeline.py
# Usage: python run_full_pipeline.py --pdf paper.pdf --provider gemini --output output_dir/
# 
# 1. Runs Phase 2: run_phase2.py --paper paper.pdf --llm-provider gemini --output <temp_dir>
# 2. Finds the model_draft.compmodel in the report dir
# 3. Runs Phase 3: run_phase3.py --phase2-report <that_dir> --output <temp_dir>
# 4. Copies model_filled.compmodel to --output
# 5. Prints the output path on stdout for Java to read
Why: The existing Phase 2 and Phase 3 are designed to be run separately with report directories. We need a single entry point the Java side can call. This script handles the glue logic and emits the final file path on stdout.
Step 1: Create the Eclipse Command Handler (Java)
Place in the existing CompartmentalModel.editor project (generated code project, but we add non-generated code in a new package):
CompartmentalModel.editor/src/compartmentalmodel/editor/actions/
├── GenerateFromPaperHandler.java    (the command handler)
├── LmlProviderSelectionDialog.java  (simple SWT dialog for provider)
└── PipelineRunner.java              (ProcessBuilder wrapper for Python)
Key flow in GenerateFromPaperHandler:
1. FileDialog for PDF (SWT.OPEN, filter *.pdf)
2. InputDialog for model name (e.g., "my_covid_model")
3. ComboSelectionDialog for LLM provider (gemini/openai/claude)
4. Eclipse Job with progress monitor:
   a. Determine paths (workspace-relative Python script)
   b. ProcessBuilder: python3 run_full_pipeline.py --pdf ... --provider ... --output ...
   c. Read stdout to get final .compmodel path
   d. Copy into workspace at CompartmentalModel/<name>.compmodel
   e. Refresh workspace
   f. Open EMF editor + Sirius diagram on the new file
Step 2: Register the handler in CompartmentalModel.editor/plugin.xml:
<extension point="org.eclipse.ui.commands">
    <category id="compartmental.editor.category" name="EpiMDE"/>
    <command id="compartmental.editor.commands.generateFromPaper"
             name="Generate Model from Paper"
             categoryId="compartmental.editor.category"/>
</extension>
<extension point="org.eclipse.ui.handlers">
    <handler commandId="compartmental.editor.commands.generateFromPaper"
             class="compartmentalmodel.editor.actions.GenerateFromPaperHandler"/>
</extension>
<extension point="org.eclipse.ui.menus">
    <menuContribution locationURI="menu:org.eclipse.ui.main.menu?after=additions">
        <menu id="compartmentalMenu" label="EpiMDE">
            <command commandId="compartmental.editor.commands.generateFromPaper"
                     label="Import Paper → Generate Model"/>
        </menu>
    </menuContribution>
</extension>
Step 3: Handle the output — open the .compmodel in the EMF editor:
// After pipeline completes and model is copied to workspace:
IFile modelFile = project.getFile(modelName + ".compmodel");
IWorkbenchPage page = workbenchWindow.getActivePage();
page.openEditor(new FileEditorInput(modelFile),
    "compartmentalmodel.presentation.CompartmentalmodelEditorID");
For the Sirius diagram, the .aird file will need a new representation descriptor added. This can be done programmatically or we can have the user right-click → "New Representation → Diagram" the first time. Let's start with manual representation creation and automate later.
Step 4: Configuration
The handler needs to know where the Python pipeline is. Approaches:
- Hardcoded relative path: ../AI-ASSISTED MODEL-DRIVEN EPIDEMIOLOGY/ (works if workspace is inside the repo)
- Eclipse preference page: user configures the path once
- System property: set via launch config
I recommend relative path for now — the repo structure already has the Compartmental/ and AI-ASSISTED MODEL-DRIVEN EPIDEMIOLOGY/ directories as siblings.
